# The executable Gherkin expression of tests/e2e/specs/24-final-e2e-sweep.spec.ts
# — the SCALED-CONCURRENT final sweep over the procedural population
# (rbac-roles.ts generateSwarm: 120 users + 4 mods + 2 admins by default).
#
# WHY THERE IS NO 126-ROW EXAMPLES TABLE. The swarm is driven as a POPULATION,
# all at once, through specs/swarm-driver.ts. Unrolling it into 126 Examples rows
# would turn one concurrent sweep into 126 sequential Playwright tests and
# DESTROY the very invariant (A) exists to prove — a serialized run peaks at 1
# actor in flight and would then "pass" 126 times. An Examples table is the
# matrix VOCABULARY (role / platform / service), not a load generator. The LAYER
# axis, likewise, is asserted INSIDE one scenario via assertControlAtLayers,
# because the point is that the SAME enacted control holds at gateway AND bff
# simultaneously — split into two scenarios it would no longer be the same
# enactment.
#
# ROLE AXIS, honestly absent. The swarm handles (e2e-swarm-mod-0,
# e2e-swarm-user-000, …) are generated by generateSwarm() and are NOT in
# SYSTEMS_SEED_USERS nor in tests/e2e-extra-personas.sh, so they are not legal
# `@role:` values — steps/dimensions.ts would (correctly) throw on them. Tagging
# a swarm handle as a persona would be a lie about who a seeder creates, so these
# scenarios declare only @layer:, and the tier is a plain Examples/prose value.
#
# HONEST GATING. `Given the live-gateway swarm preconditions hold` skips LOUDLY,
# by name, on two things: LIVE_GATEWAY!=1 and an unpopulated pre-minted JWT cache
# (SNAP_PREMINT_DIR). Both are what the lean/heavy Jobs set.
@harness @layer:gateway
Feature: Final e2e sweep over the scaled-concurrent swarm population

  # (A) The CONCURRENCY INVARIANT. Every member hits its user-floor surface at
  # once; the peak in-flight must reflect genuine parallelism. Floor 20 because a
  # serialized run peaks at 1 — pre-minted JWTs are what stop 126 actors
  # queueing behind Casdoor replicas:1.
  Scenario: the swarm population acts concurrently rather than silently serializing @layer:gateway
    Given the live-gateway swarm preconditions hold
    Then the swarm population acts concurrently with at least 20 actors in flight

  # (B)+(C) A mod REACHES the moderation surface for a user in its own assigned
  # block and is DENIED the admin plane — asserted at the gateway AND the bff
  # layer, with all mods acting at once. The mod-bff audit read has NO subject
  # filter (the only param is the boolean `mine`), so `mine=true` stands in for
  # the `?subject=` query that endpoint never supported.
  Scenario: every mod moderates its assigned block and is denied the admin plane at both layers @layer:bff
    Given the live-gateway swarm preconditions hold
    Then every swarm mod is allowed its assigned targets and denied the admin plane at the gateway and bff layers

  # (F) The CROSS-OBSERVER proof of the moderation control: not the actor's own
  # 2xx, but a SECOND principal seeing it. The mod enacts a TTL'd ban carrying an
  # audit marker, an ADMIN (different token; admin superset of moderation per
  # spec 16) reads the row back within a bounded window, and the ban is then
  # lifted so the sweep leaves no residual state.
  Scenario: an admin observes a mod's ban through the mod audit read @layer:bff
    Given the live-gateway swarm preconditions hold
    Then an admin observes a swarm mod's ban through the mod audit read within 15 seconds

  # (G) READ-AFTER-WRITE AT CONCURRENCY — the correctness half of (A)'s raw
  # concurrency floor. Disjoint author/observer pairs post simultaneously and
  # each OBSERVER (a different principal) must see its author's marker on the
  # shared local timeline within the bound.
  Scenario: concurrent author-observer pairs each achieve read-after-write @layer:homeserver
    Given the live-gateway swarm preconditions hold
    Then concurrent author-observer pairs each achieve read-after-write within 20 seconds

  # (D) NO REAL ENDPOINT — PENDING, and it is a tracked product gap, not a test
  # bug. Kept executable so it activates the day the surface exists.
  @pending
  Scenario: an admin action is announced over a per-principal SSE feed @layer:sse
    Given this scenario is PENDING because "there is no generic admin-action -> per-principal SSE feed in admin-bff-api. POST /bff/admin/reconcile does not exist, and the real SSE feeds (test-reports/{id}/events, backups/{id}/events, image-builds/{id}/events) are each scoped to ONE triggered job's id — a long-lived Flux to stream, not a since-cursor-pollable per-principal feed. None is a lightweight repeatable probe either: triggering a LEAN test-run from inside this very sweep risks recursively re-triggering the sweep, and starting a real backup or image-build has infra side effects every run. Tracked in docs/FINAL-E2E-SWEEP-MATRIX.md."
    When I open the path "/admin/events"
    Then the element with test id "admin-event-feed" is visible

  # (E) DISABLED-UI HELPTEXT — PENDING on the UI, with the framework primitive
  # (swarm-driver.ts assertDisabledHasHelptext) already written and waiting.
  @pending
  Scenario: a plain user sees the admin control DISABLED with an explaining helptext @role:e2e-user @service:admin @layer:ui
    Given this scenario is PENDING because "the host shell does not yet render role-disabled controls with helptext — a denied control is simply absent, not disabled-with-a-reason (the same honesty as spec 18's Phase 8-10 pending block). The assertion primitive assertDisabledHasHelptext(page,'nav-admin') already exists in specs/swarm-driver.ts; this activates when the role-conditional UI lands."
    Given I am signed in as the persona for this scenario
    Then the element with test id "nav-admin" is visible
